    {
      "number": 873,
      "title": "pm: composable task updates and isolated task-session context",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op pm show --json          # clean JSON, no progress chatter on stdout\nlf op pm sync                 # description-only edits no longer clobber titles\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nTwo fixes that surfaced while driving Linear from headless runs. Linear's `issueUpdate` was being sent `title: null` / `description: null` whenever only one of the two changed, so a description-only update wiped the issue title; the mutation now takes a real `IssueUpdateInput` built from just the fields present on the update. The workflow-state queries were also declaring `$teamId: String!` where Linear's schema wants `ID!`, which made status transitions fail outright. And `lf op pm show --json` was interleaving progress output with the JSON payload, so it's now run with `NullProgress` when `--json` is set — the stdout is parseable again.\n\nSeparately, tmux-launched `lf` sessions were inheriting a stale invocation context. The shell command now unsets `LF_RUN_ID`, `LF_PROCESS_ID`, `LF_HOME`, and `LF_DB_PATH` alongside the existing identity vars, and `start_lf_session_with_env` forwards the caller's values for those four unless the caller passed them explicitly. A worker launched from inside a run lands on the same db and home instead of silently picking up whatever the tmux server was started with.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `pm/linear.rs`: `UPDATE_ITEM_MUTATION` takes `$input: IssueUpdateInput!`; absent fields are omitted rather than sent as null\n- `pm/linear.rs`: workflow-state queries use `$teamId: ID!`; the query-shape test now asserts per-query rather than banning `ID!` everywhere\n- `ops/mod.rs`: `pm show --json` uses `NullProgress` so progress never lands on stdout\n- `engine/process.rs`: session shell command clears run/process/home/db-path context; explicit env wins over inherited\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 99,
      "deletions": 15,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 874,
      "title": "lf: retire the op namespace at the caller, not just in the code",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n$ lf op next\nerror: `lf op next` was removed; it has no replacement — dispatch work with `lf task run <issue-id>`, and the worker forks from and targets its parent branch\n\n$ lf op land\nerror: `lf op land` was removed; use `lf pr land`\n\n$ lf op\nerror: Removed; the operations are top-level (`lf pr`, `lf rebase`, `lf wt`, `lf pm`)\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nW2-76 asked for the deletion of `lf op next` / `advance_branch`, the `next_wave_handler` wire endpoint, and its Swift callers. All of that already landed in #872, which collapsed the runtime to waves, projects, and tasks and took the entire `lf op` namespace with it — a repo-wide grep for the rotation symbols turns up nothing but a stale memory entry and the `submit_does_not_rotate_worktree` invariant guard we want to keep.\n\nWhat the removal never did was reach the caller. Every retired spelling fell into the skill/flow catch-all and reported `skill or flow not found: op`, which tells a caller their *skill* is missing when a command namespace was retired, and names no replacement. The callers are real: older prompts, `.lf/` adaptations on other machines, agents running an installed `lf` that predates #872. That's the wave's tracked command-drift failure.\n\nThis adds a hidden `op` subcommand whose only job is to fail with the new spelling — the same shape `lf sub` already uses (`reject_retired_sub`). Nothing executes and nothing is forwarded; it's an error message with a memory, and one function to delete when it stops earning its keep.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Hidden `RetiredOp` variant in the `Commands` enum; `reject_retired_op` maps each retired verb to its survivor (`lf op pr` → `lf pr open`, `lf op rebase` → `lf rebase`, `lf op dispatch` → `lf task run`, …).\n- `lf op next` / `lf op advance` are the ones with nowhere to go: the ephemeral rotation they drove was deleted, not renamed, so the message says so and points at how work is dispatched now.\n- Parse tests covering the removal case, a renamed verb, and bare `lf op`.\n- `RELEASE_NOTES.md` mentions of `lf op` stay untouched — shipped record.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 72,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 876,
      "title": "store: namespace migrations by release, and gate `lf release` on them",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/new_migration.py add_wave_colour   # writes 0.10.002_add_wave_colour.sql\nuv run python scripts/check_migrations.py                # what CI and the release run\nlf release check                                         # now fails if a shipped migration moved\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nMigrations used to be a single `001_initial.sql` baseline with a flat ordinal, which gave no way to tell when a migration shipped and no way to catch one being edited after the fact. A shipped migration that changes rewrites the history of every database in the wild — it doesn't fix their schema, it just makes the ledger lie. This PR gives every migration a release-scoped identity (`0.10.001_initial` — the Loopflow major.minor that first ships it, plus an ordinal that restarts in each namespace) and adds a check that makes editing a shipped one a build failure.\n\nOrdering is the numeric `(major, minor, ordinal)` tuple, never a string sort, so `0.9.001` correctly precedes `0.10.001`. Databases carrying the old pre-namespace `001_initial` stamp are adopted as `0.10.001_initial` — the file is byte-identical, so nothing runs and no data moves.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `MigrationId`/`Migration` in `store/migrations.rs` own the identity: the file stem *is* the `schema_migrations.version` string, so a rename is a schema break, and one registry (`MIGRATIONS`) is the source of truth for what a database has applied. A database carrying an unknown id is told to upgrade `lf`; one that skipped a migration is told to recreate.\n- `scripts/check_migrations.py` (stdlib-only) enforces that the directory and registry agree on ids and names, that the registry is in id order with no duplicate ids, that nothing is namespaced ahead of the package version, and that no migration present at the last release tag has changed, been renamed, or disappeared.\n- `lf release check` and `lf release run` run the check themselves before cutting anything, rather than trusting the CI job of the same name — the release reads local state and never reads a CI result, so a green run isn't a gate the release actually holds. Repos without the script have no migrations to gate and skip it.\n- CI runs the same script (with full history, so the tag comparison works), and the weekly release workflow runs it before publishing.\n- `store/MIGRATIONS.md` documents the convention and what a database can be told; `scripts/new_migration.py` picks the next free ordinal and prints the registry entry to paste.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1114,
      "deletions": 52,
      "changed_files": 9
