    {
      "number": 878,
      "title": "wave chat: render the conversation, not the execution log",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf chat --follow -w my-wave        # replays the latest 12 turns, then follows live\nlf chat \"how's the build going?\"   # steer the body now playing\n```\n\nOpen the Mac app's Wave Chat tab for the same view.\n\n## Summary\n\nWave chat was showing the execution log instead of the conversation. Every tool call, shell command, file edit, state transition, and turn boundary rendered as its own line (CLI) or card (Mac), so a single long task flow buried what the wave actually *said* under twenty-odd pieces of backend evidence. This PR makes chat human-first: the thread carries speech, decisions, deliveries, and human-level failures — nothing else. The evidence still lives in the journal, where it belongs.\n\nThe CLI renderer now emits `you › …` / `wave › …` prose, drops runtime turn ids from the feed, and prints the wave's speech whole rather than ellipsized. Child activity surfaces only when a human needs it (decision required/resolved, control uncertain, PR opened, completed, failed); lifecycle churn like `state_changed` and `control_applied` stays silent. `--json` on the follow path is gone — the thread is a conversation, and the raw frames were a second view nobody used.\n\nOn the Swift side, a new `WaveChatTranscript` projection turns wire turns into conversational messages, which lets `MessageRow` shed its per-item card rendering (~180 lines) and `WaveChatView` collapse to a single conversation view. The runtime now replays a recent tail (latest 12 turns) on connect instead of the entire history, so resuming a long-lived wave doesn't dump the whole thread into the terminal.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `thread.rs`: conversational renderer; state frames and item lines removed; activity filtered to human-relevant kinds; `--json` follow mode dropped\n- `WaveChatTranscript.swift`: new projection from wire turns to conversation messages, with tests covering the same transcript the CLI pins\n- `MessageRow` / `WaveChatView`: conversation is the only view; item cards deleted\n- `runtime.rs` / `server.rs`: replay the recent tail rather than the full journal\n- README, `docs/lf.md`, wave README updated to describe what chat shows and what it withholds\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 632,
      "deletions": 408,
      "changed_files": 14
    },
    {
      "number": 879,
      "title": "release: use flat worktree names",
      "body": "Usage:\\n\\n    lf release run minor\\n\\nRelease worktrees now use flat hyphenated names such as release-default-v0-11-0, matching the worktree identity contract. This unblocks the one-shot release after the previous dotted name was rejected before a PR or tag could be created. Includes coverage for default, named, and prerelease versions.\\n\\nValidated with cargo fmt, the focused release regression test, and cargo clippy -p loopflow -- -D warnings.",
