    {
      "number": 881,
      "title": "lf status: deliver the runs and attention it promises",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf status                 # the wave this process is running inside\nlf status <wave>\nlf status <wave> --json   # runs + attention now on the wire\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n`lf status` claimed to be a wave's audit surface but only reported the Project/Task hierarchy and loop state — the runs a wave had actually produced, and the work sitting on somebody, were both missing. This adds `runs` and `attention` to `WaveDetailSnapshot`, prints them in the human view, and mirrors them in Swift so Loopflow sees the same wave.\n\nBoth land behind a new `Evidence<T>` wire enum: either `Ok { items, truncated }` or `Unavailable { reason }`. \"We looked and found nothing\" and \"we could not look\" are different facts, and a status surface that renders a failed ledger read as an empty list is lying to whoever is on call. `truncated` keeps a full page from reading as \"that was all there was\".\n\n## Changes\n\n- **runs** fold out of the same ledger and the same `summarize()` as `lf runs`, filtered to the wave — so the two surfaces cannot disagree about a run. `RunLedgerEntry` goes public. An active run is never dropped by the window cap: what a wave is doing now outranks what it did.\n- **attention** derives from the durable Session registry — a Session is waiting when its next move belongs to someone other than itself, or when its record claims a live process the machine looked for and did not find. Unstarted plan rows are not attention. Liveness is only asserted where it's observable: with no tmux, `process_alive: false` means \"unknown\", not \"gone\", and no phantom finding is raised.\n- **`lf status` with no argument** now resolves the ambient wave by `LF_WAVE_ID` as an id first (name lookup as fallback), and says the context is stale rather than \"no wave\" when the id isn't in this machine's registry.\n- `tmux has-session` probes get their stdout/stderr nulled — a missing session is the answer, not an error, and tmux was scribbling on the caller's output.\n- Swift `WaveWorkMap` gains the `Evidence` decoding, `RunLedgerEntry`, and `AttentionItem`; `wave_detail.json` fixture and the Rust/Swift fixture tests cover the new wire shape.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 903,
      "deletions": 47,
      "changed_files": 9
    },
