    {
      "number": 886,
      "title": "task PRs: keep one worktree across serial PRs",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\nStart a Task with a stable semantic worktree name:\n\n```bash\nlf task run INF-123 --name release-scoped-migrations\n```\n\nShip one PR and continue the same Task in the same worktree:\n\n```bash\nlf pr land --next released-upgrade-proof\nlf status --json\n```\n\nThe snapshot shows ordered `prs`, the `active_pr`, and the next branch after the first PR merges. Mark the final PR explicitly:\n\n```bash\nlf pr land -c\n```\n\nFor clean investigation or documentation work with no commits:\n\n```bash\nlf task complete INF-123 --summary \"Recorded the root cause\"\n```\n\nNo empty GitHub PR is opened.\n\n## Intent\n\nLet one concrete Task advance through multiple small, serial PRs without replacing its worktree, provider history, or supervision identity. Keep Task completion explicit and make the current PR's completion disposition visible to both CLI and Mac users.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Every Task PR targets `main`.\n- A Task has at most one active PR; merged and abandoned PRs remain ordered history.\n- GitHub is the current forge integration, so the publication receipt is GitHub-specific even though publication intent is not.\n- Branch rotation occurs only after the active PR is merged or abandoned and the worktree is clean.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Model only Tasks and PRs; do not add a Delivery domain.\n- Derive PR phase from publication, GitHub, merge, and abandonment evidence.\n- Nest the optional GitHub receipt inside publication so impossible state combinations do not cross the wire or enter SQLite.\n- Persist publication intent before GitHub side effects, then attach the GitHub receipt on success.\n- Keep emptiness separate from phase and skip empty PRs instead of opening them.\n- Settle a completing PR and complete its Task atomically.\n- Send ordered `prs`, `active_pr`, and `after_merge` through the Rust/Swift DTO so the UI can say when a PR completes the Task.\n\n## Not included\n\n- Parallel Task PRs.\n- Non-`main` PR targets.\n- Empty GitHub PR creation.\n- Forge integrations beyond GitHub.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3917,
      "deletions": 557,
      "changed_files": 62
    },
    {
      "number": 888,
      "title": "release: let a dispatch re-run reach the packaging fix",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# after this lands on main\ngh workflow run release.yml -f tag=v0.11.0\ngh release view v0.11.0\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n`v0.11.0` was tagged at `c0b00ff20` and dispatched, but `build-dmg` died in 1m07s on `FileNotFoundError: swift/.build/release/Loopflow` — the Mac product had been renamed to `LoopflowMac` and the DMG packager still copied the retired path. `release` and `publish-crates` gate on `build-dmg`, so nothing published: no GitHub release, no crate, no DMG in R2. The packaging bug is already fixed on `main` (#884), but every job in `release.yml` checks out `ref: ${{ env.RELEASE_TAG }}`, and the tag is immutable and predates the fix. Re-dispatching `v0.11.0` checks out the broken script and fails identically. The release is wedged: the fix exists, and the workflow cannot reach it.\n\nThis PR unwedges it. On `workflow_dispatch`, `build-dmg` checks the default branch out into `.release-tooling/` and overwrites `scripts/` with it. Application source (`rust/`, `swift/`, `python/`, `RELEASE_NOTES.md`) still comes from the tag; only the packaging tooling floats to `main`. A tag pins *what ships*, not the script that ships it. Tag-push releases are unaffected, and since a real release is dispatched from a tag freshly cut off `main`, the two trees are normally identical — the copy only bites on a re-run, which is exactly when it should.\n\nRe-entry is safe: the failure was total, so there is nothing to clean up. `build-native` re-uploads artifacts, the `release` job creates a release that does not exist yet, `publish-crates` tolerates \"already uploaded\", and the R2 upload overwrites by key.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `.github/workflows/release.yml` — two `workflow_dispatch`-gated steps in `build-dmg`: check out the default branch into `.release-tooling`, `cp -R` its `scripts/` over the tagged one. The copy covers the whole directory, not just `release-loopflow.py`, because that script imports `bundle_version.py` from the same tree — a one-file copy could pair a new script with an old helper.\n- `scratch/w2-142.md` — the recovery design, the re-entry audit, and the verification plan.\n\n## Known: v0.11.0 cannot be the terminal `latest`\n\nRepublishing the tag does not make it *sufficient*. `0.10.002_session_execution_context` landed in #882 seventeen minutes **after** the tag commit, so it is in `main` and not in the release. A fresh install is fine — an empty database applies `0.10.001_initial` and stops. But any dogfood database that has run `main` since #882 carries `0.10.002` in the ledger, and the v0.11.0 binary correctly refuses it: *\"loopflow.db was written by a newer Loopflow; upgrade lf to open this database.\"* With `make_latest: true`, it points them at itself, and there is no newer `lf` to upgrade to.\n\nThat refusal is the forward-only ledger working, not a bug — but it means **a follow-up patch release off current `main` must ship immediately** to carry `0.10.002` and honestly be `latest`. That is a separate Task; this PR does not cut it and does not move the immutable tag.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 15,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 889,
      "title": "W2-141: Now + Available work views — research, design, and the liveness fix it proved",
      "body": "Closes W2-141 (research + product design). The design doc lived in `scratch/` and is cleared at submit per the repo rule, so the durable findings are recorded here and in the three follow-on Linear tasks.\n\n## The code change\n\n`Liveness::probe` called `command_exists(\"tmux\")`, which runs `tmux --version`. tmux only accepts `-V` and exits 1 on the long flag, so the probe reported tmux as **absent on every machine** — pinning liveness to `Unknowable`, short-circuiting `is_gone()` to false, and making the attention rule *\"process is gone but the Session still records 'running'\"* dead code that had never fired anywhere.\n\nMeanwhile `process_alive` was correct all along — it's computed by `tmux_session_exists()` via `has-session`. Two liveness paths in one snapshot disagreed: one could see the process, the other believed tmux wasn't installed.\n\nNow it asks PATH. `command_exists` is untouched — its five other callers probe `gh`, which does accept `--version`.\n\n**Dogfooded before/after:** `product-performance` held a Project Session recording `running` with `process_alive: false` and raised no attention. It now reads `process is gone but the Session still records 'running'`. Across both waves the registry claimed 5 active sessions; only 2 tmux processes existed.\n\n## The design (research → decisions)\n\n**`lf status <wave> --json` already returns the whole join** — Wave → Projects (KRs + Project Session runtime) → Tasks (PM item + Task Session runtime + worktree + branch + PR + next-move owner), plus `attention` and `runs` in `Evidence` wrappers. The PM↔Session join is already done by id (`waves.rs:643-646`). So this was never a \"what data do we need\" problem.\n\n**Availability is the absence of a Session, not a PM property.** Any surface computing it from the planning store alone must reconcile against runs afterward — by *title matching*, per the docstring on the unused `RegistryQuery.backlog()`. That's the join that rots. The predicate is `!completed && runtime == None`, and both halves come from one read.\n\n**One query, two shapes.** A lens may change the *shape*, not just filter one outline:\n- **Now** — flat, cross-wave, grouped by attention (Ready for review / Needs input / Working / Stopped / Failed). This is where Claude Code's agent view landed, and Conductor — the healthiest worktree manager — uses a status-grouped list, not lanes.\n- **Roadmap** — the `Wave → Project → Task` tree from the PM snapshot. Complete, and renderable with **zero processes running**.\n\n**The row carries three orthogonal facts, never collapsed into one word:** PM completion × Session status × process liveness. Against real Product data the cross-product lands in six cells, and three are only legible as a pair — notably `done × none` (3 real rows: W2-87, W2-126, W2-114), which is \"closed outside Loopflow\", neither an error nor identical to a merged Session.\n\n**A dead process is not a dead task.** Ghost sessions aren't an error state to clean up; they're `Stopped` rows that stay attachable and resumable and say why they stopped.\n\n**No second store.** Now/Available stay client-side predicates. Obsidian Bases states the thesis: *\"There is no `from` or `source`… the filters section narrows the dataset\"* — the view holds the question, the store holds the answer. Dataview is the counter-case: a second index buys speed, constrains the query language, then goes stale.\n\n**No kanban.** The board metaphor is losing empirically (Vibe Kanban sunsetting, Crystal deprecated, Terragon shut down), and Vibe Kanban's own docs admit dragging a card *\"won't trigger any functionality\"* — a board that isn't a control surface is a lie.\n\n**Zero new verbs.** `run` / `attach` / `steer` / `resume` / `diff` / `abandon` all exist. Launch is offered exactly where `session == none` — the same predicate that defines Available. Launch and availability are the same fact.\n\n## Follow-on tasks filed\n\n- **loopflow-api** — `lf work --json`: the one machine-wide read (envelope over the existing per-wave snapshot) + a derived status category, so CLI/Mac/iOS don't each re-implement a nine-arm match. Linear, Jira and Shortcut all converged independently on splitting status *name* from a fixed status *category*.\n- **loopflow-api** — `task_sessions.issue_id` is `UNIQUE`, so **one Task gets one Session, ever**. A merged or abandoned Task can never be re-run. That's the W2-140 bootstrap deadlock one level down, and it means the roadmap will render terminal rows with no legal action.\n- **mac-surface-ux** — the Now and Roadmap surfaces over `lf work`; retires the per-wave `pm show` fan-out (one spawn, not N) and deletes the unused `backlog()` / `BacklogItem`.\n\nPlus the two that already existed: **W2-139** (truthful liveness — the Now lens is only as honest as this) and **W2-131** (all-Wave roadmap, launch/attach).\n\n## Open question, not decided here\n\n**Backlog decay.** 18 of 26 Product tasks are filed-and-unstarted, and this design makes the pile *more* visible. Linear manages backlogs by decay (auto-archive); Basecamp deletes the concept; Height automated the pruning and shut down. That's a wave-level judgment about how Product carries intent, not a view predicate — picking one silently inside a rendering task would be the wrong place. Design assumes: render everything filed, no hidden staleness filter.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cargo test -p loopflow --lib engine::process` — 4 passed, including a new test pinning the probe to agree with whether tmux actually runs. The bug survived because nothing asserted it.\n- `cargo fmt` + `cargo clippy --all-targets` clean.\n- Rebuilt `lf` and re-ran `lf status product --json`: attention went 4 → 5 items, the new one being the ghost.\n\n**Note:** 6 `journal`/`doctor` tests fail in this worktree, but they fail **identically on the base commit with my source reverted** — pre-existing, not from this change. Left alone rather than folding an unrelated fix into this PR.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 26,
      "deletions": 2,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 890,
      "title": "release: accept crates.io already-exists on publish re-run",
      "body": "## Summary\n\nThe recovered v0.11.0 run published every GitHub asset, the signed DMG, and the crate, but the aggregate workflow stayed red. Cargo now reports an idempotent publish as `already exists on crates.io index`; the workflow only recognized the older `already uploaded` wording and treated that already-published crate as a failure.\n\nThe guard now accepts both known Cargo messages while leaving every other publish error fatal. Cargo output is always printed so the release log remains diagnostic.\n\n## Changes\n\n- accept both `already exists on crates.io index` and `already uploaded`\n- keep unknown cargo-publish failures red\n- print cargo output on both idempotent and failure paths\n\n## Evidence\n\nRelease run 29369842288 published v0.11.0 successfully; `cargo info loopflow@0.11.0` downloads the crate, and the GitHub Release contains four native tarballs, Loopflow.dmg, and install.sh. This PR removes only the false-negative aggregate status for future re-entry.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 6,
      "deletions": 4,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 891,
      "title": "release: v0.11.1",
      "body": "# v0.11.1\n\nv0.11.0 collapsed the runtime to waves, projects, and tasks. This release makes the surfaces on top of it honest. Wave Chat now reads as a conversation and streams at conversational speed instead of about a word per second. A Session records the binary and store it was born with, so a supervisor can no longer relaunch it against the wrong database or restart work you already abandoned. `lf status` finally reports the runs a wave produced and the work waiting on somebody — and says so plainly when it could not look. The release pipeline that wedged on v0.11.0 is repaired end to end.\n\n## Wave Chat is a conversation again\n\nChat was rendering the execution log. Every tool call, shell command, file edit, and state transition got its own line in the CLI and its own card on the Mac, so a single long task flow buried what the wave actually *said* under twenty-odd pieces of backend evidence. The thread now carries speech, decisions, deliveries, and human-level failures — nothing else. The evidence still lives in the journal, where it belongs.\n\nThe same view was also slow, and the cause was worth measuring rather than guessing. `URLSession.AsyncBytes` yields one byte per `await`, and the chat connection is `@MainActor`, so the read loop paid a main-actor hop per byte — 0.14 MB/s. That is survivable only while frames are small, and they are not: the listener re-sends the whole open turn on every token, so a frame reaches ~106 KB and one turn puts ~68 MB on the wire. Reading in chunks pays one hop per network read instead of one per byte.\n\n- **The thread renders speech** — the CLI emits `you › …` / `wave › …` prose, prints the wave's speech whole rather than ellipsized, and drops runtime turn ids. Child activity surfaces only when a human needs it: decision required or resolved, control uncertain, PR opened, completed, failed. Lifecycle churn like `state_changed` stays silent (#878).\n- **Streaming is fast** — a single frame took ~734 ms to read byte-at-a-time and the connect replay took ~22 s; the chunked reader measures >200 MB/s. Parsing inside a chunk stays byte-level so the blank line delimiting an SSE frame still registers. Benchmarks on both sides (`cargo bench --bench wave_stream`, `swift/Benchmarks/wave_chat_render.swift`) and a test-held budget keep it there (#877).\n- **Connecting to a long-lived wave is cheap** — the runtime replays the latest 12 turns instead of the entire history, so resuming doesn't dump the whole thread into your terminal (#878).\n- **The Mac app follows** — a `WaveChatTranscript` projection turns wire turns into conversational messages, which let `MessageRow` shed its per-item card rendering and collapsed `WaveChatView` to a single conversation view (#878).\n\nNote for scripts: `--json` on the `lf chat --follow` path is gone. The thread is a conversation; the raw frames were a second view nobody used.\n\n## Sessions recover without relaunching the wrong thing\n\nA Session used to re-derive its execution context from whichever process happened to be holding it. That meant a worktree's `target/debug/lf` could relaunch a Session against the live registry — whoever typed the command chose the child's binary and store. Abandonment had a matching hole: intent was recorded when a runner *consumed* the command, not when it was queued, and in that gap a supervisor would cheerfully restart work someone had already ended.\n\n- **Execution context is pinned at birth** — `lf_bin`, `db_path`, and `lf_home` are recorded on the Session and reproduced on every relaunch. A Session created before this migration has no pinned context and none can be invented for it, so it refuses to launch and says why rather than guessing (#882).\n- **Terminal intent is durable** — `abandon_requested_at` and `abandon_reason` are written the moment the Abandon command is queued. A supervisor reading the Session sees the abandonment, not a stale `Running` (#882).\n- **tmux-launched sessions land on the right store** — the session shell command clears inherited `LF_RUN_ID`, `LF_PROCESS_ID`, `LF_HOME`, and `LF_DB_PATH`, and explicit env wins over inherited (#882).\n\n## `lf status` delivers what it promised\n\n`lf status` claimed to be a wave's audit surface but reported only the project/task hierarchy and loop state. The runs a wave had produced and the work sitting on somebody were both missing.\n\n- **Runs and attention are on the wire** — both land in `WaveDetailSnapshot`, print in the human view, and are mirrored in Swift. Runs fold out of the same ledger and the same `summarize()` as `lf runs`, filtered to the wave, so the two surfaces cannot disagree; an active run is never dropped by the window cap (#881).\n- **\"Found nothing\" and \"could not look\" are different facts** — both fields ride a new `Evidence<T>` wire enum: `Ok { items, truncated }` or `Unavailable { reason }`. A status surface that renders a failed ledger read as an empty list is lying to whoever is on call, and `truncated` keeps a full page from reading as \"that was all there was\" (#881).\n- **Liveness is only asserted where it's observable** — attention derives from the durable Session registry. With no tmux, `process_alive: false` means \"unknown\", not \"gone\", and no phantom finding is raised. Bare `lf status` resolves the ambient wave by `LF_WAVE_ID`, and reports a *stale* context rather than \"no wave\" when the id isn't on this machine (#881).\n\n## Operational notes\n\n**The v0.11.0 release was wedged, and the pipeline that wedged it is fixed.** `v0.11.0` was tagged and dispatched, but `build-dmg` died on `FileNotFoundError: swift/.build/release/Loopflow` — the Mac product had been renamed to `LoopflowMac` and the DMG packager still copied the retired path. Everything downstream gates on `build-dmg`, so nothing published. The fix existed on `main`, but every job checks out the immutable tag, so a re-dispatch checked out the broken script and failed identically.\n\n- **The packager reads the bundle** — `CFBundleExecutable` from `Info.plist` decides the copied name, covered by a regression test (#884).\n- **A dispatch re-run can reach a tooling fix** — on `workflow_dispatch`, `build-dmg` checks the default branch into `.release-tooling/` and overwrites `scripts/` with it. Application source still comes from the tag; only packaging tooling floats to `main`. A tag pins *what ships*, not the script that ships it. Tag-push releases are unaffected, and a real release is dispatched from a tag freshly cut off `main`, so the copy only bites on a re-run — exactly when it should (#888).\n- **An idempotent crate publish is not a failure** — Cargo now reports a re-publish as `already exists on crates.io index`; the workflow recognized only the older `already uploaded` wording and turned a successfully published crate into a red run. Both are accepted, every other publish error stays fatal, and Cargo output is always printed (#890).\n\n**Schema.** This release ships one migration, `0.10.002_session_execution_context`, adding the pinned-context and abandon-intent columns to `task_sessions` and `project_sessions`. It applies on first run; existing rows keep NULL context and refuse to relaunch rather than guess. A patch release appends to the active `major.minor` namespace, which is why a 0.11.x migration is numbered `0.10.*` — the namespace is chosen when the migration is authored, and migrations authored after a minor bump begin the next one (#885).\n\n## Small changes\n\n- The `wave/README.md`, `swift/README.md`, and migration docs track the shipped contract rather than the intended one (#885, #878).\n- `RegistryQuery` and the `wave_detail` DTO fixture carry the new evidence fields across Rust and Swift in lockstep (#881).\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 80,
      "deletions": 79,
      "changed_files": 6
    },
    {
      "number": 892,
      "title": "docs: remove retired commands from active infrastructure memory",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nrg -n \"lf op|lfd\" wave/infrastructure/MEMORY.md rust/loopflow/src/engine/builtins/LOOPFLOW.md\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nKeeps active infrastructure prompt context on the shipped top-level CLI after v0.11. Historical release notes and decision ledgers remain unchanged.\n\n## Changes\n\n- replace retired `lf op` spellings with current top-level commands\n- describe current sibling-only `lf wt create` contract\n- remove obsolete `lfd`, pre-Session worktree, and pending migration instructions\n- verify builtin `LOOPFLOW.md` has no stale operation spellings",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 13,
      "deletions": 16,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 893,
      "title": "lf ssh: bound noninteractive cron-host probes",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf ssh mini-heart -- lf cron list\n```\n\nAn unreachable or misconfigured host now fails fast with a host-named error instead of hanging indefinitely.\n\n## Summary\n\n`lf ssh` spawned a bare `ssh <host> bash -s` with no transport bounds, so probing an unhealthy cron host (`lf ssh mini-heart -- lf cron list`) would hang past 60s with no output — a host-key or password prompt reads from the controlling tty rather than our already-EOF piped stdin, so it waits forever. This silently defeated the cron-host health probe. The fix adds standard noninteractive-SSH bounds and classifies the exit so transport failures surface clearly while real remote command codes still propagate. Credential preamble and secret handling are untouched, and `-A` stays opt-in.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `run_ssh` now passes `BatchMode=yes` (the primary hang killer — never prompt for password/host-key/passphrase), `ConnectTimeout=10`, and `ServerAliveInterval=10`/`ServerAliveCountMax=3` to bound both the connect handshake and a stalled established session.\n- New pure, unit-tested seams: `ssh_args(host, forward_agent)` builds the bounded arg vector, and `classify_exit(code)` maps `255`/signal death to a sanitized, host-named `ConnectionFailure` error while propagating real remote command codes verbatim.\n- The connection-failure message names the host and connection/transport phase without leaking any credential value; ssh's own reason stays visible on inherited stderr.\n- Tests cover the arg bounds, agent opt-in, exit classification, and the sanitized error message.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 117,
      "deletions": 11,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 894,
      "title": "project sessions: create successors after terminal pursuit",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\ncargo nextest run -p loopflow\n```\n\nOn a copy of a registry with an abandoned Project Session, run `lf project run <project>`. The old Session remains queryable, one pinned successor is created, and `lf project status <session-id> --json` shows `predecessor_session_id` / `successor_session_id`.\n\nVerified with clippy, 1,184 Rust tests, the end-to-end smoke suite, and a backup of the live registry.\n\n## Intent\n\nFix W2-140: the documented recovery path said to abandon an unsafe legacy Project Session and pursue the same Linear Project again, but the schema permanently reserved that Project id. Terminal pursuit now becomes append-only history while exactly one non-terminal successor owns current work. Task and Project launch races converge through the same partial unique index.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- `0.11.002` follows the landed task-PR migration and rebuilds only `project_sessions`.\n- The migration runner already disables foreign-key actions and validates the rebuilt graph before commit; tests prove Project events survive the rebuild.\n- Project lookups resolve the newest Session. An explicit `ps_...` id reads history, and status exposes both neighboring identities.\n- Failed, blocked, waiting, and running Sessions remain current; only completed or abandoned Sessions open a successor.\n\n## Not included\n\nNo Task multi-PR behavior changes and no automatic relaunch of unsafe legacy provider history.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 318,
      "deletions": 56,
      "changed_files": 10
    },
    {
      "number": 895,
      "title": "Wave Home: a user-owned execution address + the shared control path",
      "body": "## W2-88 — Wave Home: a user-owned execution address + the shared control path\n\nA Wave's **Home** is a user-owned execution address — an *owner* plus a\n*location* — that every surface resolves, probes, starts, and routes through one\nshared Loopflow API. Not a host alias, not a local/remote boolean, not an lfd\ndaemon or a new Concerto protocol.\n\n### Address model — `engine/wave_home.rs`\n`WaveHome { owner, location }`. Canonical `jack@local` and `ssh://jack@host[:port]`;\nshorthand `jack@host` normalizes to the SSH URI. DNS, IPv4, bracketed IPv6, and\noptional ports flow through one `parse` funnel and one `Display` formatter. The\nowner is **required** (no implicit current-user Home) and distinct from\ncredentials, which keep riding SSH + Doppler. Reachability is never enforced at\nparse time — public IP, private/Tailscale DNS, and public host are the same\nlocation; whether it answers is operational evidence.\n\n### Control path — `ops/home.rs`\n- `probe_home` resolves a Wave→Home and classifies **Unreachable | Stopped |\n  Running | Unknown** with a reason and, when running, the attach endpoint. Local\n  reads the endpoint directly; remote routes `lf status --json` through the Home's\n  SSH address (`capture_routed`).\n- `start_home` is **idempotent**: it probes first and returns a running\n  resident's identity instead of launching twice; a reachable-but-stopped Home is\n  started **on the Home** (local: detached tmux `lf wave`; remote: the same over\n  SSH), then re-probed for the attach identity.\n- Everything routes through the Home address and the existing SSH/credential\n  machinery — no surface reimplements SSH or owns a parallel lifecycle.\n\n### Evidence on the shared DTO\n`WaveSnapshot.home` carries the address (owner + structured location);\n`WaveDetailSnapshot.home_runtime` (`HomeRuntimeDto`) carries state + reason +\nattach endpoint + the one contextual `HomeAction` — **Attach** when running,\n**Start on `<home>`** when stopped, the reason otherwise. `lf status <wave>`\nprobes the focused Wave; `lf ls` stays address-only (no per-wave SSH). Swift\nmirrors the DTOs (`RegistryQuery.swift`) and the fixture round-trips.\n\n### CLI / transport / routing\n- `lf home probe <wave>` and `lf home start <wave>` (`--json`) — the shared\n  surface a conductor calls without implementing SSH.\n- `ssh` transport carries `owner@host` + `-p port`; `capture_routed` feeds the probe.\n- Repo/PR/release/PM commands in a remote-home Wave forward over `lf ssh`;\n  `LF_HOME_ROUTED` breaks the forward loop. Project/Task launches inherit the\n  Home via `LF_WAVE_HOME`.\n- `docs/wave-authoring.md`: Home frontmatter + `lf home` usage.\n\n### Verified\n- Unit: address parse/format for every form; owner-required rejection; IPv6\n  bracketing; probe classification (running/stopped/null-registry/garbage→unknown);\n  start session naming; DTO round-trips; routed preamble + `-p`; action-follows-state.\n- End-to-end with the built binary: `owner@host` routing forwards to the SSH\n  destination (bounded fast-fail); local Home runs in-process; `lf home probe`\n  prints `jack@local [stopped] → Start on jack@local` and the matching `--json`.\n- `cargo fmt`/`clippy` clean; touched-module tests green (6 pre-existing lib\n  failures are a shared-store DB-version mismatch, unrelated).\n\n### Downstream — Mac Now / Roadmap (not in this PR)\n- **Conductor UI**: the Open/Attach/Start button, Home navigation, showing a Wave\n  at `jack@local` / `ssh://jack@host`. The shared contract is delivered; the\n  pixels consume it. Replace `MacLocalWaveAgentLauncher`'s tmux lifecycle with\n  `lf home start`.\n- **Remote attach transport**: the SSH tunnel to a remote resident's loopback\n  endpoint (the shared API returns the identity; the UI owns the tunnel).\n- **Remote cold-start live coverage**: the remote detached `lf wave` launch is\n  constructed and unit-tested but not yet verified against a live host (Cron-host\n  bootstrap earns that).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1489,
      "deletions": 31,
      "changed_files": 18
    },
    {
      "number": 896,
      "title": "lf cron sync: install a wave's repo-owned schedules as launchd jobs",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf cron sync --wave infrastructure   # reconcile launchd jobs to match GOAL.md crons:\nlf cron list                         # prove what's installed\n\n# bring a host up end-to-end (bounded lf ssh, secret-free, idempotent)\nscripts/bootstrap-cron-host.sh mini-heart infrastructure\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nStands up a maintained Mac mini cron host (mini-heart) that runs `lf` on a schedule via launchd — no daemon or resident wave listener required. Schedules are declared once in the wave's `wave/<wave>/GOAL.md` frontmatter (`crons:`), and `lf cron sync` reconciles the host's launchd jobs to match that declaration: it installs (or replaces) a job per declared cron, prunes jobs for the wave whose flow is no longer declared, and reports schedules launchd can't run. Running it twice against the same declaration is idempotent.\n\nlaunchd's `StartCalendarInterval` only expresses a repeating wall-clock time, so `sync` accepts only fixed-daily schedules: it parses the cron expression with the `cron` crate, takes two consecutive fires, and requires them exactly 24h apart. Sub-daily, weekly, and multi-time expressions are skipped with a reason rather than silently mismapped — the resident listener still honors the full expression.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `lf cron sync --wave <wave>` command reconciling launchd jobs against `GOAL.md` `crons:`.\n- `sync_crons`, `schedule_from_cron`, and `daily_time_of` in `ops/cron.rs`, with `CronSyncResult`/`SkippedCron` result types; `Schedule::Daily` becomes `Schedule::DailyAt { hour, minute }` so the plist renders the declared time.\n- `release/CRON_HOST.md` documents the host, prerequisites, repo-owned schedules, and v0 limitations (host-local timezone, daily-only shape).\n- `scripts/bootstrap-cron-host.sh` probes reachability/auth, checks `lf` + Doppler, syncs, and lists — idempotent and secret-free.\n- Tests cover daily-time extraction, non-daily rejection, install/prune reconciliation, and skip-on-unschedulable.\n- Provisioning against mini-heart is blocked until the host answers on the tailnet; the repo-committable half is done and verified locally (see `scratch/questions.md`).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 392,
      "deletions": 9,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 897,
      "title": "wave stream: delta-granular live-turn wire",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf thread follow          # watch a wave's live turns grow, delta by delta\n```\n\nThe stream reconstructs each open turn from a whole-`turn` frame plus a run of small `turn-delta` increments, so the wave's speech renders token-by-token without a whole turn crossing the wire per token.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe live-turn wire re-broadcast the entire accumulated `ChatTurn` on every content delta — O(prose²) bytes over a turn. This replaces that with delta-granular frames: a whole `turn` frame opens (and re-baselines) a turn at the O(1) events — opening, body-session update, finalization — and each in-turn content increment rides a small `turn-delta` frame carrying just the new `ConversationItem`. Clients absorb each delta into their reconstructed open turn with the same fold the listener uses, so the reconstruction matches the served turn exactly. When the stream lags, a `resync` frame drops the reconstruction and the reconnect's whole-turn replay rebuilds it cleanly. The wire now carries O(fragment) per token.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `TurnDelta` DTO (`turn_id` + `item`) with a round-trip fixture under `tests/fixtures/dto/`, mirrored in Swift (`ChatTurn`/`WaveChatClient`)\n- Runtime broadcasts a `TurnBroadcast` enum (`Whole`/`Delta`) over a single channel so frame order and the lag counter stay unified; frames stay `Arc`-shared for one serialization across N subscribers\n- `lf thread follow` reconstructs open turns from `turn`/`turn-delta`/`resync` frames and breaks the connection on `resync` to trigger a fresh replay\n- Server emits delta frames on content increments and heals gaps with `resync`\n- `follow`/`stream_events` callbacks return `ControlFlow` so a reader can break the stream\n- Tests cover delta growth, tool-item routing, resync-drops-reconstruction, wire amplification, and the updated `wave_stream` bench for the broadcast enum",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 703,
      "deletions": 90,
      "changed_files": 14
    },
    {
      "number": 898,
      "title": "supervision: BodyObservation state model + projection (W2-135 PR1)",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow child_session\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA Session is durable intent; a body is the disposable process acting for it. This PR gives that distinction a type: `BodyObservation` records what the *current body* is observed doing (Working, Stalled, Recovering, NeedsInput, Stopped, Failed, Terminal, Unobservable), separate from the Session's durable `status`. The observation is *derived*, never stored — a pure `observe()` projection over durable intent, whether the body is alive, and how long since it last made meaningful progress. No second monitor store.\n\nLiveness (a body exists) and progress (it advanced) are separate inputs, and `Working` vs `Stalled` is exactly their difference. Terminal intent dominates every signal, and an unobservable body is never asserted gone. Each category carries its evidence, the owner who must act next (Session/Loopflow/Human/Nobody/Unknown), and the legal controls a surface may offer — one shape every surface (CLI, Mac, iOS, chat, Now/Roadmap) reads.\n\nThis is PR1 of W2-135: the read model only. The precise per-flow progress deadline arrives with the write lease in a later PR; until then `DEFAULT_STALL_AFTER` (30 min) is the honest coarse bound the projection can prove.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `child_session`: `BodyCategory`, `BodyOwner`, `BodyControl`, `BodyObservation`, `BodyIntent`, `BodyEvidence`, and the clock-free `observe()` projection, with unit tests covering the Working/Stalled threshold, unobservable, stopped-not-gone, and terminal/failed/waiting rows\n- `ProjectSessionStatus` and `TaskSessionStatus` each gain `body_intent()`, coarsening durable status onto the shared `BodyIntent` so one projection serves Project and Task Sessions alike",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 373,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 899,
      "title": "pm: give every wave its own Linear team and Task prefix (1/2 — binding + init)",
      "body": "First of two serial PRs for W2-155. This one is the **forward-cut**: bind each\nwave to its own Linear team so new work gets wave-scoped identifiers\n(PRD-*, INF-*, INT-*) instead of the shared W2-*. Existing issues are untouched.\n\n## Why this works\nA Task's identifier prefix is derived by Linear from the **team** its issue\nlives in — nothing in loopflow generates `W2`. Every wave shared one\nmachine-global team (`config.linear.team`), so everything was W2-*. Reads follow\nInitiative → Project → Issue and are team-agnostic, so binding a new team never\ndisturbs a wave's existing issues; the team only steers *creation*.\n\n## What's here\n- `pm.linear_team` in GOAL.md frontmatter — the stable team **id** (identity);\n  the key (`PRD`) stays mutable presentation.\n- `lf pm init --team-key <KEY>` adopts or creates the wave's team, **diagnosing**\n  a key owned by another team or a name reused under a different key instead of\n  guessing. Idempotent: a fully-bound wave is a no-op with zero network touch;\n  an initiative-linked wave (today's state) just gains a team.\n- `build_client` resolves the wave's team, falling back to `config.linear.team`\n  for unbound waves (no regression for anything not yet migrated).\n- `lf pm doctor` flags waves missing a team binding and any two waves sharing a\n  team.\n\n## Tests\nUnit: `ensure_team` adopt/create/conflict paths (mock Linear), GOAL round-trip\nfor `linear_team`, `resolve_team` wave-over-config precedence, default key\nderivation, and the new `--team-key`/`--team-name` parse. `cargo clippy --tests`\nclean.\n\n## Not here (PR 2/2)\nThe `lf pm reteam` migration that moves existing settled issues to per-wave\nteams, protecting active Sessions and preserving completed W2 history. See\n`scratch/give-every-wave-its-own.md`.\n\n## Deliberate deferral\nThe live proof (real PRD-/INF-/INT- task creation) is a human rollout step —\nrunning `lf pm init` creates real Linear teams, and doing it mid-Task on the\nlive product wave could interfere. The wiring is unit-proven; the rollout is a\ndeliberate external action.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 484,
      "deletions": 46,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 900,
      "title": "lf roadmap: machine-wide intent view and Task reference contract",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf roadmap                 # planned work across every Wave on this machine\nlf roadmap --wave product  # scope the same snapshot to one Wave\nlf roadmap --json          # wire snapshot for Loopflow (Mac/iOS)\nlf status product          # live execution + attention for one Wave\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds `lf roadmap`, the machine-wide intent plane: every Wave's durable local plan joined to whatever live Task/Project execution evidence exists, bucketed into attention sections (`now`, `needs_attention`, `available`, `later`). Sections are derived once in Rust and stamped on the wire so CLI, Mac, and iOS bucket identically without re-deriving the rule. Alongside the command, the builtin skills that report on more than one Task now share a single reference contract, and `task_clarify` gains a computable design contract so a Task's finish line is an observable outcome, not a pile of implementation receipts.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`lf roadmap` command** — new `RoadmapSnapshot` / `WaveRoadmap` / `RoadmapProject` / `RoadmapSection` wire types (required-or-Optional, no serde defaults), wired through `lf.rs` and `waves.rs`. Reuses the leaf snapshots `lf status` already emits.\n- **Shared Task reference** — `wave_pursue`, `project_pursue`, `scan`, `assess`, and `wave-report` skills all render Tasks as `[identifier](provider URL) — active PR/workspace slug — status/next owner`, sourced strictly from roadmap/status JSON with no reconstructed URLs, branches, or slugs.\n- **Computable design contract** — `task_clarify` requires user-visible outcome, end-to-end proof, source of truth, affected surfaces, absent/error states, operational boundary, and exclusions before leaving clarify.\n- **`TaskReferenceSnapshot` / `TaskWorkspaceSnapshot`** — stable issue-URL + workspace evidence that outlives a Task's process and final PR; moved worktree/branch off the runtime snapshot.\n- **`tmux_live_sessions`** — batched `list-sessions` lookup so callers checking many sessions pay one fork instead of one `has-session` per name.\n- **PM `url` field** on `PmItem`, plumbed through Linear and the ops output tests.\n- **Swift + DTO fixtures** — `WaveWorkMap`, `RegistryQuery`, task/wave detail views updated; new `roadmap_snapshot.json` fixture and `wave_detail.json` changes with matching fixture tests.\n- **README** — documents the `lf status` / `lf roadmap` / `lf pm` split.\n\nDesign note under `scratch/make-lf-work-the-machine.md`.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1504,
      "deletions": 117,
      "changed_files": 23
    },
    {
      "number": 901,
      "title": "sessions: lease provider bodies across handoffs",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task resume INF-123 --model codex --reason \"Claude quota exhausted\"\nlf project resume <project-slug> --model codex --reason \"Claude quota exhausted\"\n```\n\nPlain `resume` continues the same provider transcript. `--model` keeps the Task or Project Session, its directive, worktree, and active PR chain, but hands the next body generation to another agent. It refuses while another body is still writing — interrupt that body first.\n\n## Summary\n\nA durable Session and the provider process running it are now cleanly separated. The provider process plus its transcript is a replaceable \"body generation\"; the Session — identity, directive, worktree, supervision state, PR history — persists across handoffs. This lets a Task or Project survive provider failure or a rate limit by resuming under a different agent without losing durable work, instead of being pinned to the model it started on.\n\n`ChildProcessGeneration` is reframed as a write lease: `generation` is a monotonically increasing fencing token, and only the current generation may act for the Session, so a replacement body advances the token rather than assuming the old body's identity.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `lf task resume` and `lf project resume` gain `--model` and `--reason` (reason requires model); the ops layer threads the handoff request through to the store.\n- New `ChildBodyHandoffRequest` and `ChildBodyHandoff` types; `TaskEventKind::BodyHandedOff` / `ProjectEventKind::BodyHandedOff` emit typed audit events surfaced in chat activity.\n- Session status display shows `body:` (generation + agent + provider) in place of a bare provider string.\n- SQLite child-session store reworked to persist and advance body generations across handoffs.\n- Docs (README, `docs/lf.md`, `docs/waves.md`, troubleshooting, flowloop README) updated to describe bodies as replaceable generations and point rate-limit recovery at `resume --model`.\n- Minor: `InboxAction::Deliver` now boxes its `InboxItem` to shrink the enum.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 922,
      "deletions": 142,
      "changed_files": 18
    },
    {
      "number": 902,
      "title": "pm: lf pm reteam — migrate existing issues into per-wave teams",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf pm reteam --wave designer            # dry run: names every issue it would move, defer, and leave\nlf pm reteam --wave designer --apply    # move the settled open issues into the wave's team\n```\n\n`lf pm doctor` flags issues still stranded in the old team, and `lf pm sync` surfaces the same as a diagnostic.\n\n## Summary\n\nOnce a wave is bound to its own Linear team, its older issues still live in the shared team. `lf pm reteam` migrates them. It **defaults to a dry run** — printing every issue it would move (with the renumbering, e.g. `W2-155` → `PRD-<next>`), every one it defers, the completed issues it leaves as historical, and the target team — and only mutates with `--apply`.\n\nThe move is safe because Linear keeps the stable issue UUID across a team change, so Session/PR/comment links survive even though the number is reassigned. Reteam is conservative and idempotent: it defers any issue whose Task has a non-terminal Session (running, waiting, blocked, failed, …) to protect live or in-review work, leaves completed issues in the shared team as historical (shipped `W2-*` references are immutable, so renumbering buys nothing), and skips issues already carrying the target team key. Each moved issue gets a comment recording its prior identifier.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `PmCommand::Reteam { wave, apply }` subcommand wired through `run_pm`, with a dry-run-by-default result printer.\n- `pm_reteam` in `ops/pm.rs`: classifies each issue (move / defer / historical / already), executes moves via a new `move_item_to_team` Linear mutation, comments the old identifier, and refreshes the snapshot.\n- Pure `classify_reteam_item` / `identifier_has_team_prefix` helpers, unit-tested for the move/defer/leave/skip matrix and prefix-collision guard.\n- `lf pm sync`/`doctor` now report open issues stranded outside a team-bound wave's team as reteam candidates.\n- README documents the command and its safety guarantees.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 526,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 903,
      "title": "sessions: fence child bodies with explicit write leases",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task status W2-135 --json \\\n  | jq '.latest_process | {generation, agent, provider, state, outcome, tmux_name, pid, process_group_id}'\n```\n\nThe generation receipt now exposes the current provider, lease state, terminal outcome, tmux/PID, and owned process group for a Task or Project Session — while the write-lease token stays private to the runner and never surfaces in status or events.\n\n## Summary\n\nOnly one body may write for a durable child Session (Task or Project) at a time. This PR makes that ownership explicit with a per-generation write lease: the runner mints an opaque `ChildLeaseToken` plus generation number, hands it to the provider body via env (`LF_TASK_LEASE_TOKEN`/`LF_TASK_GENERATION` and the Project equivalents), and every store write that mutates a child body — appending events, delivering/accepting/failing commands, reconciling stale deliveries — is now gated on validating that lease. A stale body whose lease has been revoked or superseded can no longer clobber the record. The lease token implements neither `Serialize` nor `Display`, and its `Debug` is redacted, so an error path can't leak it into logs, events, or status.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `ChildWriteLease` / `ChildLeaseToken` capability types plus `ChildLeaseState` (legacy/reserved/active/revoked/finished) and `ChildBodyOutcome` (completed/interrupted/failed/lost/superseded/legacy-stopped).\n- `ChildProcessGeneration` receipt gains `agent`, `provider`, `provider_session_id`, `process_group_id`, `state`, and `outcome`; runners record the observed provider and reap the owned process group.\n- `ChildTarget` carries its lease; `apply_input` and interrupts validate the lease before touching the harness, and store command mutations move to `*_for_lease` variants.\n- New `BodyLeaseChanged` Task/Project event rendered into chat activity.\n- Migration `0.11.003_child_body_lease.sql` and expanded sqlite store logic to persist and validate leases.\n- `docs/waves.md` documents the generation receipt and the private write-lease token.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3945,
      "deletions": 559,
      "changed_files": 20
    },
    {
      "number": 904,
      "title": "mac: add all-wave roadmap controls",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```text\nOpen Loopflow → All Repos → Roadmap\nStart, attach, resume, or interrupt a Task from its roadmap row.\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nMake the default Wave Chat pane consume the shared `lf roadmap --json` snapshot across every registered Wave. Durable plan rows remain visible for stopped Waves, per-Wave read failures retain their evidence, and whole-query refresh failures keep the last successful roadmap on screen.\n\n## Changes\n\n- render the canonical Wave → Project → Task hierarchy with its server-derived sections\n- route Wave open/start and Task run/attach/resume/interrupt controls through existing lifecycle commands\n- reuse the Task workspace for agent attachment, diffs, files, shells, PRs, and worktree opening\n- cover roadmap action selection and exact lifecycle argv with the shared DTO fixture\n\n## Verification\n\n- `swift test -Xswiftc -gnone` — 103 tests\n- `uv run python ../scripts/check_swift_multiplatform_boundaries.py`\n- macOS `xcodebuild build-for-testing`\n- live `lf roadmap --json` read across seven live/stopped Waves, including one explicit unavailable plan",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 782,
      "deletions": 42,
      "changed_files": 8
    },
    {
      "number": 905,
      "title": "gate: bound every phase and split off the required UI host gate",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/test.py --all         # bounded full gate; prints elapsed / budget per phase\nuv run python scripts/test.py --list --all  # the plan and every phase budget, run nothing\nuv run python scripts/test.py --ui-host     # the required hosted LoopflowUITests run (permissioned host)\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nMakes `scripts/test.py --all` a bounded and honest local release gate. Every phase now runs under a printed wall-clock budget (`PHASE_BUDGETS`); an overrun is killed—process group and all—and reported as `TIMEOUT <phase> (budget Ns)`, so no phase can hang the gate. The summary states what each suite actually proves, closing the gap where the `loopflow` suite looked like it exercised UI behavior when it only compiles the app and UI-test runners. The real hosted `LoopflowUITests` run is split off into a separately named **required** gate (`--ui-host`) that never runs under `--all` because it needs macOS UI-automation permission; a missing permission is reported as a named `MISSING CAPABILITY` failure with a next action, never a silent skip. On failure, phase logs and any `.xcresult` are preserved under `.lf/tmp/gate/run-<pid>/<suite>/` for one-command repair without opening Xcode.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Per-phase wall-clock budgets with SIGTERM→SIGKILL group-kill on overrun; unlisted phases fall back to `DEFAULT_BUDGET_S`.\n- New `ui-host` required gate: runs `xcodebuild test -only-testing:LoopflowUITests`, with a precheck that fails fast on non-macOS or a simulated capability gap (`LF_UI_HOST_SIMULATE_NO_PERMISSION`), and a classifier that treats runner-bootstrap failures as capability gaps while keeping genuine assertion failures raw.\n- `Suite` gains `proves`, `host_gate`, `precheck`, and `classify` so the summary never over-claims.\n- `release/GATE_BUDGET.md` documents the budgets and a headless CI measurement (run 29396456085); `release/UI_HOST_GATE.md` documents the maintained host, the permission it needs, and how absence is reported.\n- `test_gate_bounded.py` covers timeout-kill, group-kill, actionable failures, missing tools, `--all` never running the host gate, and the capability-classification paths.\n- `TESTING.md` and a CI comment cross-reference the two-level UI split and the budget doc.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 567,
      "deletions": 29,
      "changed_files": 6
    },
    {
      "number": 906,
      "title": "runs: separate skill runs from process execs",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf runs\nlf status --wave core\nlf execs\nlf trace <exec-id>\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nMake runs mean agent-backed skill invocations, where context, model, token, cost, and outcome evidence belongs. Wave status filters that same dataset, while process-level orchestration diagnostics move to execs and trace accepts an exec id.\n\n## Changes\n\n- add `lf execs` for the raw process ledger\n- report skill runs with context and token totals in `lf runs` and `lf status`\n- make `lf trace <exec-id>` reconstruct the containing process tree\n- mirror the run DTO in Swift and cover the split with behavior tests",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 642,
      "deletions": 200,
      "changed_files": 17
    },
    {
      "number": 907,
      "title": "pm doctor: detect a Project stranded on a foreign team",
      "body": "",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 119,
      "deletions": 17,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 908,
      "title": "lf store: isolate dev and release control-plane stores",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf doctor            # prints build provenance, resolved DB path, latest known + applied migrations\n\n# Development builds resolve their own per-worktree store:\n#   ~/.lf-dev/worktrees/<source-identity>/loopflow.db\n# Release builds share the production store:\n#   ~/.lf/loopflow.db\n\n# Break-glass: point a dev build at the production DB\nLF_ALLOW_PRODUCTION_DB_FROM_DEV=1 lf ...\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nBuilds now carry an embedded provenance (`development` or `release`) so a branch checkout can no longer migrate the production store or another worktree's dev store. `build.rs` stamps provenance and a source-root identity into the binary; CI release workflows set `LOOPFLOW_BUILD_PROVENANCE=release` explicitly. Development builds resolve a store under `~/.lf-dev/worktrees/<source-identity>/loopflow.db` derived from the checkout path, and refuse `~/.lf/loopflow.db` even when it arrives via `LF_DB_PATH` unless `LF_ALLOW_PRODUCTION_DB_FROM_DEV=1` is set. Session runners pin their binary, home, and store through internal `LF_CONTROL_BIN`/`LF_CONTROL_HOME`/`LF_CONTROL_DB_PATH` variables, and provider agents no longer inherit `LF_BIN`, `LF_HOME`, `LF_DB_PATH`, or the break-glass flag. Before advancing an existing on-disk database, migrations now take an exclusive lock and write an atomic backup named for the previously applied migration.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `build_info` module: embedded provenance + stable per-checkout source identity, with a `build.rs` emitter and validation\n- Store resolution splits dev vs release paths; control-plane env vars pin session binary/home/db and are scrubbed from vendor agent environments\n- Atomic pre-migration backup under an exclusive transaction (`rusqlite` `backup` feature, `fs2` file lock)\n- `lf doctor` reports provenance, resolved DB path, and latest known/applied migrations, even when the DB is too new or from a divergent dev build\n- CI release/nightly/weekly workflows stamp `LOOPFLOW_BUILD_PROVENANCE=release`\n- Test support and new `test_environment_tests` isolate storage while preserving temp-home config; docs (`architecture.md`, `lf.md`, `MIGRATIONS.md`) updated",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1488,
      "deletions": 179,
      "changed_files": 33
    },
    {
      "number": 911,
      "title": "mac: add the NOW work lens over one roadmap read",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nswift test --package-path swift --filter WorkAttentionTests\n# then launch the Mac app with every wave stopped — the default surface is NOW\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nPR 1 of W2-146: give the Mac a **NOW ⇄ ROADMAP lens** over the single `lf roadmap --json` read, so a user sees all current and available work across every wave in one surface with zero wave processes running. NOW is a flat, cross-wave list grouped by who must move next — *Ready for review, Needs input, Working, Stopped, Failed* — ordered oldest-first within each group. ROADMAP keeps the existing Wave › Project › Task tree. Both shapes are pure projections over the same snapshot; `generated_at` stays on screen so staleness is visible. This ships the headline \"see all current and available work in one surface\" with no new Rust and no wire change.\n\nWork rows carry the three orthogonal facts on independent channels — PM completion, Session status, process liveness — never collapsed into one word. A Stopped row (dead process, non-terminal Session) stays attachable/resumable and says why it stopped: a dead process is not a dead task. Ineligible rows are greyed with their reason attached, never hidden.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `WorkAttention.swift` in the shared `Loopflow` package: `nowGroup(for:)` — a pure, total bucketing function over shared roadmap fields (`section`, `next_move.owner`, `runtime.status/process_alive`, `active_pr`, `task.completed`) — plus `NowRow`/`NowSection` flattening, oldest-first ordering, empty buckets dropped. Predicate lives in the shared package so iOS/CLI can reuse it verbatim.\n- `RoadmapView`: `WorkLens { now, roadmap }` toggle, header retitled \"Work\", flat NOW rendering, `roadmapTaskIsActionable` greyed-with-reason discipline.\n- New `WorkAttentionTests` proving each Task lands in the expected group off the shared fields.\n- Deletes `RegistryQuery.backlog()`, `BacklogItem`, the `items` field on `PmShowSnapshot`, and `RegistryQueryTests.backlogDecodesOpenTasks` — availability is the absence of a Session, not `!completed` over a title-match join.\n- Design doc `scratch/w2-146.md` (removed on land).\n\nHome control (wiring `home_runtime` + `lf home start` onto the Wave row) is PR 2 and out of scope here.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 511,
      "deletions": 88,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 912,
      "title": "wt list: make inspection side-effect free",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf wt list          # inspect worktrees without touching canonical main\nlf wt list --sync   # explicitly fetch and fast-forward main first\n```\n\nRun `git status --short` before and after bare `lf wt list`: local edits, HEAD, refs, and the stash remain unchanged.\n\n## Summary\n\nMakes `lf wt list` a side-effect-free inspection command. It previously called `sync_main` unconditionally, which could fetch, hard-reset, and auto-stash the canonical checkout merely to render a list. That made a read command capable of disrupting Wave and Project control-plane work.\n\nFreshness is now explicit: bare `lf wt list` reports against the last-synced main, while `--sync` opts into fetching and fast-forwarding before the read.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Add `lf wt list --sync` as the explicit mutating path.\n- Keep bare `lf wt list` read-only.\n- Document the freshness behavior in CLI help.\n- Prove bare list preserves HEAD, working tree/index, stash, and refs.\n- Prove `--sync` performs the requested fast-forward.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 111,
      "deletions": 4,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 913,
      "title": "pr: reconcile out-of-band merges + add lf pr next",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# A PR merged out of band (GitHub auto-merge, not `lf pr land -c`) and follow-up work remains:\nlf pr next            # reconcile the merge, rotate to the next serial PR, carry uncommitted edits forward\nlf pr next my-slug    # name the next serial branch explicitly\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAn out-of-band merge (GitHub auto-merge armed by `lf pr land`, not settled by `lf pr land -c`) used to strand a Task in a state no advertised command could repair: a stray open/draft sibling on the branch could hide the merge, and follow-up edits sat uncommitted with no clean way to rotate to the next serial PR. This PR adds three narrow, testable fixes proven by two dogfood incidents (W2-116/#905 and W2-166/#907): reconcile now prefers the merged PR over noisier siblings, `lf pr next` rotates the Task forward while carrying dirty edits, and the scratch stash moves under the ignored `.lf/tmp` prefix so it stops dirtying the worktree.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`lf pr next [slug]`** — new command that reconciles an out-of-band merge into the settled PR, then rotates the worktree to sequence N+1, carrying preserved follow-up edits forward. Refuses if the current PR isn't merged yet or the Task is terminal.\n- **Reconcile prefers the merged PR** — `select_reconcile_pr` ranks merged > open/draft > closed (newest as tiebreak), so an out-of-band merge is never hidden by a stray sibling left behind by a land.\n- **Rotation carries dirty trees on demand** — `RotateOptions { carry_dirty, slug_override }` splits the runner's strict clean-tree rotation from the operator's `lf pr next`, which carries uncommitted edits and can name the next branch.\n- **Scratch stash under ignored prefix** — moved from `.lf/scratch-stash/` to `.lf/tmp/scratch-stash/` so it no longer dirties the worktree and blocks `lf task complete`.\n- **Guidance** — task-pursue skill and runner task seed now advertise `lf pr next` for the out-of-band-merge case.\n- Design doc under `scratch/` (removed on land).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 253,
      "deletions": 13,
      "changed_files": 8
    },
    {
      "number": 914,
      "title": "stacked Tasks: place child worktrees on the active parent PR",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n    lf task run CHILD --stack-on PARENT\n\nPARENT must have an active published PR. CHILD gets its own readable sibling worktree and branch forked from that PR. `lf pr open` targets the parent automatically.\n\nThe selector binds to the active PR at launch. Later serial PRs in PARENT do not move CHILD.\n\n## Delivery\n\n- Persist the exact parent Task PR and fork commit.\n- Rebase child-only work when the parent changes.\n- Refuse child land before the parent merges.\n- After merge, collapse child-only commits onto main and retarget the PR.\n- Keep same-Task multi-PR delivery serial through `lf pr land --next` and `lf pr next`; remove `lf pr stack`.\n\n## Proof\n\n- Migration namespace check\n- Rust PR, land, and rebase integration tests\n- Clippy with warnings denied\n- Prompt golden tests",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 798,
      "deletions": 47,
      "changed_files": 35
    },
    {
      "number": 915,
      "title": "resolve: one ambient-wave resolver for every lf command",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# From a resident wave's environment (LF_WAVE_ID set to the durable UUID):\nlf status          # resolves the ambient wave\nlf pm show         # resolves the SAME wave — no longer ignores the env\nlf pm status\n\n# Explicit --wave still wins everywhere:\nlf pm show --wave product\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nEvery `lf` command that acts on \"the wave I'm inside\" now reads the ambient wave through one shared resolver instead of each consumer inventing its own rule. The bug (W2-151) was never that a resident exports `LF_WAVE_ID=<uuid>` — that's correct — but that consumers disagreed on how to read it: `status` handled a UUID, `pm show` ignored the env entirely, and others silently dropped a hand-set name. This introduces `resolve_managed_wave_name` (and a sync, store-free variant for `lf pm`) with one rule: explicit `--wave` wins, else `LF_WAVE_ID` as a durable registry UUID mapped to its wave's name, else a hand-set name used directly, else a classified error. A UUID the registry has never seen is a loud `StaleIdentity` error rather than being silently re-read as a name.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `wave_context`: new `WaveResolveError` (NoContext / StaleIdentity / UnknownExplicit / Registry) and the `resolve_managed_wave_name` async resolver plus `resolve_managed_wave_name_sync` for sync PM call sites\n- `lf pm`: every arm resolves the ambient wave through the shared helper; `NoContext` preserves the old \"all waves / pass --wave\" behavior, a stale id errors loudly\n- `lf status`: `resolve_status_wave` delegates to the shared resolver, then requires a registry row (a wave with no row has no runs to report)\n- Tests: a resolver matrix across all seven ambient environments, plus end-to-end proof that `lf status` and `lf pm show` agree from a resident wave's environment\n- Design + open questions captured under `scratch/`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 379,
      "deletions": 41,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 916,
      "title": "task ci-fix: give a waiting task's open PR a CI-derived next move",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf status <wave-id>\n```\n\nFor a task whose PR is open, the reconcile path now reads GitHub's required checks and `lf status` reports a CI-derived next move: **CI** owns it while required checks are pending or failing, then it hands back to **Review** once they go green. Failing reasons name the offending checks, e.g. `required checks failed: build, lint`.\n\n## Summary\n\nThis persists a per-head CI observation on a task's PR so a waiting Task doesn't burn a body waiting on checks it can't see. On the reconcile path, when a PR is open we capture its head SHA and read `gh pr checks --required` to classify the required checks into passing/pending/failing (a `cancel` bucket counts as failing; any failure dominates pending). That reading is stored against the head commit and cleared when the PR merges or closes. `next_move_for_task` then routes an open PR by its fresh CI reading — pending/failing → CI owner, passing → Review — and falls back to plain review waiting when CI state is unknown (no head, no required checks, or gh unavailable). This lays the groundwork for waking a waiting Task into a `ci-fix` skill.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `CiObservation`/`CiState`/`CiCheck` on `TaskPr`, persisted via migration `0.11.004_task_pr_ci_state` and the store layer\n- `PrInfo`/`GithubPr` now carry `head_sha` (`headRefOid`), threaded through create/reconcile/attach\n- `required_check_state` in `ops::pr` reads and classifies required checks; unknown state is never a hard error on reconcile\n- `lf status` surfaces the CI owner and names failing checks; new tests cover the CI routing and check classification\n- Design notes and open questions under `scratch/`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 537,
      "deletions": 28,
      "changed_files": 12
    },
    {
      "number": 917,
      "title": "gate: make --ui-host re-runnable and classify the hung-runner permission gap",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/test.py --ui-host          # runs the required hosted-UI gate\nuv run python scripts/test.py --ui-host          # ...and again — no longer dies on a stale result bundle\n\n# simulate the permission gap without Xcode\nLF_UI_HOST_SIMULATE_NO_PERMISSION=1 uv run python scripts/test.py --ui-host\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAttempting the 5/5 real-host proof for the required `--ui-host` gate surfaced two genuine defects, both fixed here. First, `_ui_host_commands` wrote to a **fixed** `-resultBundlePath`; `xcodebuild test` exits 64 rather than overwrite an existing bundle, so every run after the first died in ~1s. The bundle is now pid-scoped under `_run_artifact_root()`, making the gate re-runnable. Second, on macOS 26 / Xcode 26 the UI-automation permission denial surfaces as a hung control session (`Timed out … initiating control session with daemon` / `The test runner hung before establishing connection`, exit 65) rather than a clean TCC denial — a signature the classifier didn't recognize, so a permission gap misreported as a red test. That signature now classifies as `MISSING CAPABILITY`, verified against the real captured log.\n\nThe 5/5 green run stays open: on `Jacks-MacBook-Pro` both real-host attempts hung before `LoopflowUITests` executed, because the host hasn't granted the runner Automation + Accessibility. That approval is an interactive TCC dialog and can't be granted headlessly, so the gate now honestly reports the gap instead of a false failure.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Per-run pid-scoped `-resultBundlePath` so `--ui-host` re-runs instead of dying on a stale bundle (exit 64)\n- Hung-control-session signature classified as `MISSING CAPABILITY`, not a red test\n- Two new tests: hung-control-session classification, and per-run (not fixed-path) result bundle\n- `release/UI_HOST_GATE.md`: documents the macOS 26 / Xcode 26 denial signature and adds a verification log with the one operator action to unblock 5/5\n- `scratch/ui-host-runs/`: real-host run logs and driver capturing whether `LoopflowUITests` actually executed",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 86,
      "deletions": 4,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 918,
      "title": "mac: Home control on the wave row over lf home probe/start",
      "body": "## Usage\n\nOpen the Loopflow Mac app and look at any Wave card in the roadmap. Each row now shows its Home address, a probed liveness chip, and one contextual action:\n\n- **Open** — the resident is running; attaches to its endpoint\n- **Start on `<home>`** — reachable but stopped; runs `lf home start` on the *configured* Home\n- an actionable reason — when the Home can't be reached\n\n```bash\nlf home probe <wave> --json\nlf home start <wave> --json\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThe Wave row's old \"Start & open\" button launched the wave locally via `LocalWaveAgentLauncher`, which was wrong for any Wave whose Home is a different machine. This replaces that button with a `WaveHomeControl` that renders the shared `HomeRuntimeDto` contract: it probes the Home once per card (on appear, keyed to the Wave — never per row and never on the 15s roadmap poll), shows liveness, and offers the single action the DTO dictates. The app never does SSH itself — `lf home probe` classifies the Home (local reads instant, remote routed over credential-forwarding) and `lf home start` idempotently starts the resident on its configured Home, returning the attach identity. `openWave` is now pure navigation; starting is the control's job.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `RegistryQuery`: add `homeProbe` and `homeStart`; make `HomeState`, `HomeAction`, `HomeRuntime` public and add the new `HomeStartResult` DTO (`wave`, `started`, `runtime`)\n- `RoadmapView`: new `WaveHomeControl` view (address + state chip + contextual action); drop the local-launch path from `openWave` and the `activeControlId` start button\n- Tests: decode a `stopped` probe into its Start action, and decode a `started` result into its running attach identity",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 193,
      "deletions": 40,
      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 919,
      "title": "receipt: bind curated memory facts to evidence receipts",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# Bind a fact to the records that justify it (repeatable for many-to-one evidence)\nlf memory add \"workers report via the stream\" \\\n  --receipt chat_turn:turn-3 \\\n  --receipt pr:loopflow/loopflow#912@abc1234\n\n# Read facts back with their receipts as JSON\nlf memory log --json\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nCurated claims — `MEMORY.md` facts, KR proofs, executive Wave reports — are human-readable summaries that, left alone, become a second unaccountable truth. This PR introduces the `Receipt`: a small, stable pointer from one curated claim to the one canonical raw record that justifies it (a chat turn, worker report, trace turn, PM change, or PR), keyed by that record's own durable id rather than a line number or session id. Receipts survive the perturbations curation outlives — Markdown edits, branch deletion, session succession, and database migration. This first slice wires receipts through memory facts: `lf memory add --receipt kind:reference` parses and stamps evidence at the CLI boundary, the live server journals each receipt alongside its fact, and `lf memory log --json` reads the receipt-bearing view back for drilling.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `receipt` module: `Receipt` / `EvidenceKind` wire types (no serde defaults), `kind:reference` parsing with per-kind opaque references and a `run` alias for `worker_report`\n- `lf memory add` gains a repeatable `--receipt` flag; parse errors surface as user errors at authoring time\n- `lf memory log --json` emits facts with their receipts, read directly from the local journal (receipts are journaled, not carried on the live prose stream)\n- Journal `MemoryAdded` events now carry `receipts`; `append_memory` and `write_memory` thread them through\n- Swift `Receipt` model + DTO fixture (`tests/fixtures/dto/receipt.json`) keeping the Rust/Swift wire types in lockstep\n- Design doc under `scratch/` describing the receipt contract",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 563,
      "deletions": 39,
      "changed_files": 14
    },
    {
      "number": 921,
      "title": "auth: route provider OAuth accounts",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf auth connect claude --account primary\nlf auth connect claude --account reserve\nlf auth use claude primary\nlf auth accounts claude\nlf ssh mini -- lf wave product\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nRoute Claude and Codex OAuth sessions across operator-owned accounts while preserving provider session continuity, shared Loopflow skills, and process-lifetime SSH credential forwarding.\n\n## Changes\n\n- add private native provider profiles with deterministic health-aware selection, cooldowns, preferences, and session pins\n- expose account connect, list, use, enable, disable, reset, and disconnect commands\n- consume Claude and Codex rate-limit frames and restart cooled sessions on a healthy account\n- forward only locally refreshed access-token leases over SSH stdin, with no remote auth files or refresh tokens\n- preserve the existing single-account behavior when no managed accounts exist\n\n## Verification\n\n- cargo test -p loopflow\n- cargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings\n- cargo fmt --all -- --check",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2811,
      "deletions": 102,
      "changed_files": 18
    },
    {
      "number": 922,
      "title": "mac: trajectory & evidence section — record and inspect wave memory",
      "body": "## Usage\n\nOpen a Wave's detail on the Mac. Below its projects, a new **Trajectory** section shows the Wave's curated memory facts oldest-to-newest, each with its evidence receipts as `kind:reference` chips (a `pr:` receipt is a clickable GitHub link). Record a new note inline:\n\n```bash\n# CLI write → Mac read round-trips within one refresh\nlf memory add \"trajectory: chose X over Y\" --receipt pr:loopflow/loopflow#918@57ca881 -w product\n\n# Mac write → CLI read: what you Record on the Mac shows up here\nlf memory log --json -w product\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis is PR 1 of the W2-86 arc — conducting a Wave end to end from the Mac. It ships the smallest coherent conduct slice: read a Wave's curated trajectory, inspect the evidence behind each note, and record a new evidence-backed note, round-tripping to the same state `lf memory` shows in a terminal. Reads are daemon-less journal folds (`lf memory log --json`), so the trajectory renders whether or not the Home is live; the write goes through the Wave's live Home (`lf memory add`), the sole holder of MEMORY.md's pen, so a stopped Home surfaces a Start-on-Home hint instead of failing silently. There's no Mac-only domain model — the section decodes the shared `MemoryFact`/`Receipt` Codable types and renders. Later serial PRs on this branch add calibration approval, beat scheduling, the `lf receipt show` drill, and iOS parity.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `Receipt.browserURL` resolves a `pr:` receipt (`owner/repo#N`, optional `@sha` pin dropped) to a GitHub pull-request URL; other kinds render token-only until the receipt drill lands.\n- `RegistryQuery` gains `memoryLog(wave:cwd:)` (daemon-less read against the wave's origin repo) and `memoryAdd(wave:fact:receipts:cwd:)` (shells `lf memory add`, surfacing the no-server error).\n- New `WaveTrajectorySection` / `TrajectoryNoteView` inside `WavePlanView` — facts list, receipt chips, compose field with a Home-liveness gate, and RoadmapView-style refresh/error idioms; polls on the plan pane's existing refresh signal.\n- DTO fixture test coverage for the receipt/memory-fact wire shape.\n- Design note under `scratch/` scoping PR 1 and naming the remaining serial slices.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 270,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 923,
      "title": "Context Lab: research and refine real session context",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n1. Select the `product` Wave and open **Context Lab** from its header.\n2. Open **Sources** and select `headless surface` or `wave_pursue`.\n3. Confirm main's current file fills the primary pane beside impression and\n   revision evidence.\n4. Choose a Refinement Project if prompted.\n5. Click **Refine in task-worker** only when ready to create a real Task.\n\n## Intent\n\nContext Lab makes the text shaping a Wave's agent sessions inspectable and\neditable. It separates initial-prompt load, lifetime provider input, and peak\nrequest pressure, then pairs those measurements with a Sources worklist ranked\nby agent-session impressions.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Context Lab opens from a selected Wave, with repo and Wave fixed for the\n  window's lifetime. It is progressive disclosure from the primary Wave UI,\n  not a global destination.\n- Selecting a source opens main's current file as the primary object;\n  historical revisions and representative trace addresses remain evidence.\n- Rust owns session reconciliation, source identity, impressions, provider\n  normalization, and coverage. Swift renders one atomic snapshot and owns\n  interaction state.\n- **Refine in task-worker** creates and starts a normal Project-owned Task and\n  opens the existing Task workspace on its Agent view. Single-Project Waves\n  route automatically; multi-Project Waves remember an explicit Refinement\n  Project.\n- The handoff refreshes the Wave plan and Context Lab snapshot, validates the\n  source receipt, then rechecks main's source immediately before Task creation.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- An impression is one distinct agent session whose captured initial prompt\n  contains the source at least once.\n- Missing provider or source-level capture stays unavailable rather than\n  becoming zero.\n- Cost remains outside Context Lab; context-window pressure is the comparable\n  capacity signal.\n\n## Not included\n\n- No embedded Markdown editor, alternate agent host, synthetic quality score,\n  or hidden Task lifecycle.\n- Automated review stops before the Task-creation click. The resulting live\n  agent session, source diff, and backlink are the human-review checkpoint.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Full repository gate passed after rebasing onto current main.\n- Python: 59 passed.\n- Rust: formatting and clippy passed; 1,418 tests passed, 2 skipped.\n- Website: 59 passed, 3 skipped.\n- Swift: 133 tests across 23 suites passed; multiplatform boundaries passed.\n- E2E smoke passed; Xcode app/UI-test `build-for-testing` passed.\n- Migration order and checksums passed through `0.11.012`;\n  `git diff --check` passed.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 7092,
      "deletions": 332,
      "changed_files": 36
    },
    {
      "number": 924,
      "title": "Task PR range: prove M==B parity + guard placement at the source",
      "body": "## What\n\nGuarantees every Task PR contains only that Task's work (W2-138), on two fronts:\n\n**Publication proof (backstop).** `verify_task_pr_range` gates `ops/land.rs::prepare_pr` before the first GitHub side effect. The invariant is `M == B` where `M = merge-base(origin/<default>, HEAD)` and `B` = the recorded `TaskPr.base_commit`:\n- `M == B` — GitHub range == `lf task changes`; publish.\n- `M < B` — recorded base carries commits absent from origin (the #877/#882 contamination); **refuse before push**, naming the foreign commits/files and the `git rebase --onto` recovery.\n- `B < M` — stale/squash-merged base; heal `base_commit → M` (dedicated `heal_task_pr_base` store write, since base_commit is optimistic-identity in the generic update).\n- divergent — refuse as ambiguous.\nNo remote falls back to local `<default>`.\n\n**Placement guard (source).** `resolve_upstream_base` anchors placement + serial rotation on fetched `origin/<default>` (explicit local fallback with no remote); `refuse_if_canonical_ahead` stops `task_run` when canonical main is ahead of its upstream — the root cause of #877/#882.\n\n## Proof\n\n10 unit tests over all five Proof scenarios (parity, contamination-refuse, stale-heal, squash-merged-parent-heal, no-remote, rotated continuation) plus placement (origin-anchor, no-remote fallback, ahead-of-origin refusal, in-sync allow). land/pr/worktree integration suites green.\n\nConsumes `TaskPr {base_commit, sequence, branch}` — no new stack/PR-state model (W2-93/W2-172 boundary).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 445,
      "deletions": 14,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 925,
      "title": "revert mac trajectory and evidence section",
      "body": "## Usage\n\nBuild and open the Mac app, then select a Wave. The Trajectory section introduced by #922 is absent; existing Wave detail, Now, Roadmap, and Home controls remain unchanged.\n\n## Summary\n\nReverts #922 in full. The trajectory-and-evidence interaction was not the desired Mac conduct model, so this removes that product direction cleanly before further W2-86 work builds on it. The broader Task remains open for redesign.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Remove the Wave Trajectory section and note composer.\n- Remove the branch-specific memory log/add query helpers.\n- Remove PR-receipt browser URL projection added only for that surface.\n- Remove the corresponding DTO fixture assertions.\n\nVerification: swift test --package-path swift passes all 108 tests.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 0,
      "deletions": 270,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 926,
      "title": "auth: trust Claude browser callbacks",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf auth connect claude --account primary\n```\n\nAuthorize in the browser; no paste-code prompt appears, and the command completes from Claude callback confirmation.\n\n## Summary\n\nStop treating UUIDs in Claude OAuth URLs as device codes and make auth subprocess ownership cancellation-safe.\n\n## Changes\n\n- parse user codes only from explicit URL fields or code-prompt text\n- cover the real Claude callback URL shape with a regression test\n- terminate the provider process group when auth is cancelled or Loopflow exits\n\n## Verification\n\n- cargo test -p loopflow\n- cargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings\n- cargo fmt --all -- --check",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 170,
      "deletions": 21,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 929,
      "title": "pull-local-bin: fix empty-args expansion under macOS bash 3.2",
      "body": "bash 3.2 treats \"${args[@]}\" as unbound under set -u when the array is empty, so the script died on line 65 whenever run with no flags. Guard the expansion with ${args[@]+...}.",
      "files": [],
