      "changed_files": 3
    },
    {
      "number": 949,
      "title": "pr: separate headless publication from review presentation",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf pr publish --title \"...\" --body \"...\"   # push + create/update PR, print state+URL, no browser\nlf pr open --title \"...\" --body \"...\"      # publish, then open the PR for human review\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nSplits the two things `lf pr open` used to conflate: publishing a PR (push, create-or-refresh, write title/body) and presenting it for review (opening the GitHub page in a browser). Publication is now the headless default that agents, flows, and automation use; presentation is an explicit, human-initiated action. The old behavior — every PR command silently launching a browser — meant headless runs spawned browser windows and a failed launch could make a successfully published PR look failed. Now a failed review-surface launch fails only `lf pr open`, and the published PR and its URL are untouched.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `lf pr publish` command: push + create/update, print state + URL, opens no browser. This is the agent's default \"make a PR\" verb.\n- `lf pr open` now publishes through the shared path, then presents for review; it's the one command that opens a browser.\n- New `ops/present` module with a `ReviewSurface` enum (`GithubBrowser` default, `#[non_exhaustive]` for future terminal/file surfaces) as the single presentation boundary.\n- Removed implicit `open_url` calls from `ops/pr.rs` and `ops/land.rs` so publish and land no longer launch browsers.\n- Flow `op:` steps run headless — both `publish` and `open` only publish.\n- `ship` flow and builtin skills/docs now reach for `pr publish`; `pr open` is reserved for when a human explicitly asks to see the PR.\n- Tests confirming submit/land/publish make no presentation attempt and that `pr open` presents once, plus a `counting_open_script` test-support helper.\n- Docs (README, getting-started, ops) and LOOPFLOW.md updated to describe the publish/open split.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 482,
      "deletions": 100,
      "changed_files": 27
    },
    {
      "number": 951,
      "title": "task review: add durable parent dialogue protocol",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow interaction_review --lib\ncargo test -p loopflow store::migrations::tests::a_fresh_database_applies_the_whole_chain_once --lib\ncargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings\n```\n\nThe protocol test opens one headless Gate review, proves replay idempotency at the same lifecycle waitpoint, conducts two FIFO parent questions plus a Task reply, records an authorized disposition, and then proves a later Gate epoch creates a fresh review.\n\n## Intent\n\nGive interactive Task steps one durable conversation and decision record whether the reviewer is a human or the owning Project. This is the transport boundary that lets headless execution preserve the same review exercise instead of skipping it.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- A Task review belongs to one exact phase epoch, iteration, and step cursor.\n- The owning Project is the only agent reviewer for a Task; attended completion follows separately.\n- Parent questions and final disposition must enter the existing Task provider session as ordered follow-ups, never steer replacements.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Fence both writers with their active Task or Project generation lease.\n- Persist the review request and its full evidence snapshot in the Task event observed by the Project.\n- Wake the Project only for the request and Task-authored replies; reviewer echoes and completion do not self-wake or leak into the root Wave.\n- Queue the final disposition back to the Task so approval and changes-requested survive provider timing and restarts.\n\n## Not included\n\nTask-runner routing, attended human handoff, `--headless` launch UX, skill rewrites, and Wave catch-up are stacked follow-ups.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1702,
      "deletions": 14,
      "changed_files": 15
    },
    {
      "number": 952,
      "title": "task review: route headless exercises through projects",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow headless_interactive_step_opens_parent_review_with_current_evidence --lib\ncargo test -p loopflow task_follow_up_is_fifo_and_never_interrupts --lib\ncargo test -p loopflow --lib\ncargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings\n```\n\nThe headless runner test creates a real temporary Git repository, enters an interactive Gate step, and proves the Task opens one Project review carrying the current branch, base, head, worktree fingerprint, skill instructions, and exact lifecycle waitpoint. Full verification passes 1,230 library tests.\n\n## Intent\n\nRun the same interactive lifecycle exercises without requiring a human to be present. A headless Task delegates each interactive kickoff, iterate, and gate step to its owning Project; it never skips the skill or invents a synthetic approval.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Keep the Task flow body open at its playhead cursor until the durable review completes.\n- Separate provider-turn activity from flow-turn activity so Project questions can reach an idle Task session as ordered follow-ups.\n- Recover requested, active, and completed reviews after process restart without opening a second waitpoint.\n- Apply `changes_requested` from Gate immediately by returning the same Task Session, provider transcript, worktree, and PR to Iterate.\n- Expose `lf task run|start --headless`, and allow an existing idle Task to defer its remaining interaction policies.\n\n## Review notes\n\n- Project questions and dispositions are persisted before delivery and are never steer replacements.\n- Task status names the active Project review and its durable state.\n- The standard policy remains human kickoff, Project-deferred iterate, and human gate; `--headless` changes only the reviewer routing for all three phases.\n\n## Not included\n\nAttended Human review transport, Project-to-Wave reviewer routing, interactive skill rewrites, and manual Wave catch-up follow separately.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 591,
      "deletions": 67,
      "changed_files": 9
    },
    {
      "number": 953,
      "title": "task: block and resume a parent behind an interactive handoff",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\n# an agent opens an interactive handoff mid-turn; its parent Task parks on a human\n# once the human finishes:\nlf handoff complete <session-id>   # resolves the handoff and wakes the blocked parent\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen a Task agent opens an interactive handoff (something only a human can do — an OAuth login, a manual gate), the parent Task now parks on that handoff instead of pushing forward, and resumes exactly once when the human resolves it. The durable handoff row is the sole source of truth for \"this parent is waiting on a human,\" and the flow cursor never advances past the step that opened the handoff until the handoff is terminal and its wake is claimed. Because every body re-derives its obligation from these two durable records at birth, process death, app restart, and host restart all replay to the same rendezvous rather than losing or double-running it.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `task::interactive_rendezvous` module: reads the parent's handoffs and reconciles the current rendezvous (`None` / `Waiting` / `Resume`), claiming the terminal wake for the running generation so resume happens exactly once.\n- `runner.rs` reconciles the rendezvous at body birth (park without starting a provider turn if a human is still needed) and also parks mid-turn when the agent opens a handoff during a completed turn, clearing the active body as interrupted so the interactive step stays current.\n- `lf handoff complete` now wakes the blocked parent Task after recording the terminal outcome — best-effort, since the handoff is already durable and a failed wake must not fail the human's completion.\n- `ops::task` splits out `resume_task_async` so callers already inside a runtime (the handoff-completion wake) can resume a Task without a nested `block_on`.\n- Scratch notes documenting the rendezvous design, the PR-creation blocker, and the store-resolution root cause.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 551,
      "deletions": 22,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 954,
      "title": "W2-176: Show Active Sessions in a new Control destination",
      "body": "Adds the first user-visible **Control** destination and its **Active Sessions** view — a machine-wide census of every live Wave, Project, Task, direct-execution body, and interactive handoff, grouped by Wave. Chat stays the default Wave experience; Control is reached from one Wave-header button.\n\n## What's here\n- **lf handoff list [--active] [--parent] --json** + InteractiveHandoffListRow DTO — completes the handoff contract (Store::list_interactive_handoffs had no CLI). Round-tripped in Rust and Swift; carries no argv/env, so Open re-fetches the attach descriptor and keeps first-attach evidence at lf handoff attach.\n- **ActiveSessionsCensus** — a pure projection over lf roadmap + lf runs + lf handoff list, indexed by the ids each contract declares (no Swift joins, no filesystem inference). Owns red propagation (a waiting handoff reddens its Task, Project, and Wave while the body is alive), evidence classification (observed/stale/stopped/unreachable/unavailable/missing stay distinct), and view-only-vs-openable controls. A mixed fixture pins every rule.\n- **ControlView + ActiveSessionsView** — only interactive handoffs expose Open (to lf handoff attach then embedded Ghostty); every other body is view-only. Run History is a quiet deferred affordance. No Swift-owned Session lifecycle.\n- Narrow shell edit: one header button + one sheet in WaveDetailPane, so W2-178 keeps the information hierarchy.\n\n## Verification\ncargo test -p loopflow (1232 passed), clippy + fmt clean, swift test (143 passed) on the rebased tree. Live lf roadmap / lf runs reads confirmed against the production store; the live lf handoff list census check runs once this branch builds post-merge.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1783,
      "deletions": 3,
      "changed_files": 16
    },
    {
      "number": 955,
      "title": "skills: support parent reviewers when headless",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task <name> --headless\ncargo test -p loopflow interactive_skills_support_human_and_parent_reviewers\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nHeadless Tasks defer interactive inner-loop steps to their owning Project, but the interactive builtin skills only described attended, human-driven behavior. This teaches each interactive skill both modes: the launch prompt names the reviewer, and every skill now defines a **Human reviewer** path (the existing conversation) alongside a **Parent reviewer** path that conducts the same exercise independently, routes questions and answers through the durable review protocol, and never blocks on an unavailable human. Parent reviewers make evidence-backed decisions and request changes with concrete findings rather than implementing the Task's fixes themselves.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Added a `## Reviewer mode` section to the interactive skills: `code-review`, `demo`, `design`, `explore`, `refine`, `review-design`, `review-open-work`, `review`, and `init`.\n- `demo` gains a \"Prove every Done When\" evidence matrix — pick the surface (product, code/tests, admin state, logs, stats/metrics) that most directly proves each claim, and exercise a real sign-in path for auth work instead of seeded or mocked state.\n- Phrasing throughout distinguishes human-confirmed decisions from parent-reviewer judgment (e.g. `review` labels its summary; `review-open-work` renames \"Discuss\" passes to \"Resolve\").\n- New test asserting each interactive builtin defines both reviewer modes, routes through the review protocol, and that `demo` enumerates its evidence surfaces.\n- README note that interactive skills define both attended and parent-reviewer behavior in one durable review conversation.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 275,
      "deletions": 15,
      "changed_files": 13
    },
    {
      "number": 956,
      "title": "handoff: resume the same step when work returns",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf handoff hand-back <session-id> --summary \"Finish the remaining review fixes\"\n```\n\nThe parent resumes the interrupted flow step and receives the human summary as its next message. `complete` remains distinct: it means the human finished the whole interactive step, so the parent advances.\n\n## Review\n\n- Keeps flow position owned by the durable Task session.\n- Keeps `InteractiveHandoff` presentation/execution semantics separate from lifecycle review truth.\n- Adds behavior tests for cursor preservation and summary delivery.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cargo test -p loopflow handed_back --lib`\n- `cargo test -p loopflow hand_back_resumes --lib`\n- `cargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 88,
      "deletions": 16,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 957,
      "title": "task review: conduct attended lifecycle gates",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf task run DES-123\nlf task status DES-123\nlf task attach DES-123\n\nlf task review message ir_... \"Show the real sign-in path\"\nlf task review complete ir_... \\\n  --disposition changes-requested \\\n  --outcome \"The login proof is missing\"\n```\n\nStandard Tasks now conduct interactive kickoff and gate exercises with the human in the Task provider session. Bare attached input and `task review message` are FIFO follow-ups; `/interrupt` remains explicit. Approval advances the lifecycle. Gate changes return the same Task to Iterate. Headless Tasks keep routing the same exercises to the owning Project.\n\n## Durable boundary\n\n- Each attended exercise is an `InteractionReview` at an exact Task flow position.\n- The existing Task provider session stays alive across human questions and answers.\n- Restart recovery reopens the same review with its full Task and skill context.\n- A generic `InteractiveHandoff` may present or wake the work, but cannot approve a lifecycle review or advance past one.\n- Human reviewer authority is rejected from managed Task, Project, and Wave agent environments.\n\n## Review findings\n\nThe structural review separated presentation from judgment: handoffs own human-only actions; InteractionReviews alone own approval and return-to-Iterate. A final follow-up is drained before Gate settlement so it cannot leak into the next phase.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `cargo nextest run --all` — 1463 passed, 2 skipped\n- `cargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings`\n\nIncludes the correction from #956 while that PR clears the merge queue; GitHub will remove the duplicate diff when main advances.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1042,
      "deletions": 178,
      "changed_files": 9
    },
    {
      "number": 958,
      "title": "reviews: run manual Wave catch-up exercises",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf reviews catch-up --wave product --plan\nlf reviews catch-up --wave product --skill demo\nlf reviews catch-up --wave product --skill code-review\n```\n\n`--plan` prints the exact evidence packet without launching an agent. The live commands gather every completed and still-open parent-reviewed InteractionReview in the Wave, attach Task/Project/commit/worktree/PR/disposition/outcome evidence, and launch one attended human exercise over the integrated state.\n\n`demo` requires a proof matrix for each relevant design-doc Done When using product, code, admin state, logs, stats, or metrics, with real sign-in/login when auth is in scope. `code-review` reviews the combined architecture and integration seams.\n\nCatch-up is deliberately not lifecycle authority: it cannot rewrite source Task dispositions. New findings become follow-up Tasks. The command routes to the Wave Home so the evidence and worktrees are local to the reviewer.\n\n## Review findings\n\nThis stays manual and receipt-free for the prototype. It rereads all durable parent reviews on every invocation, making no claim that debt was cleared merely because a session launched.\n\n## Verification\n\n- catch-up prompt behavior and bounded skill selection tests\n- generated CLI flag-table test\n- Wave Home routing test\n- `cargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 314,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 959,
      "title": "auth: import Codex logins and bind email-less Claude",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n    lf auth import codex --account engineering\n\n## Summary\n\nAdopt an active ChatGPT OAuth login into a private managed Codex home without repeating browser authentication. Claude profile connection now uses the selected Chrome email when current Claude Code omits its login email, while preserving strict mismatch rejection and strict Codex identity checks.\n\n## Verification\n\n- 13 focused auth and credential tests\n- cargo fmt --check\n- cargo clippy -p loopflow --all-targets -- -D warnings\n- live managed Codex import and controlled two-account takeover through the staging registry",
